    },
    {
      "id": "wLRK45PqHG875b5gKjh3J",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 832.0539503593751,
      "y": 718.8383222857143,
      "width": 594.53955078125,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0C",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1565719373,
      "version": 1423,
      "versionNonce": 303262520,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793192263774,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Comtrol flow = the path a thread takes through the code \n(which branch an if-else takes). \n\nSIMD works well when all threads in a warp follow\nthe SAME path: one instruction, all 32 take effect.\n\nControl divergence = threads in the same warp follow \nDIFFERENT paths.\n\nThen the hardware takes multiple passes, one pass per path:\npass 1 = then-path (A), pass 2 = else-path (B).\n\nIn each pass the threads on the other path are inactive;\nthe instruction is still executed for the whole warp,\nbut only the threads on this pass's path are allowed to\ntake effect. So every thread appears to take its own path.\n\nCost = the extra passes + the execution consumed by\nthe inactive threads in each pass.\n\nOne can determine if a control constructor can result in\nthread divergence by inspecting its decision condition. If\nthe decision condition is based on threadIdx values,\nthe control statement can potentially cause thread\ndivergence.\n\nExamples of thread divergence are if statements, such as\nif(threadIdx.x > 2) or if a loop condition is based on\nthread index values.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Comtrol flow = the path a thread takes through the code \n(which branch an if-else takes). \n\nSIMD works well when all threads in a warp follow\nthe SAME path: one instruction, all 32 take effect.\n\nControl divergence = threads in the same warp follow \nDIFFERENT paths.\n\nThen the hardware takes multiple passes, one pass per path:\npass 1 = then-path (A), pass 2 = else-path (B).\n\nIn each pass the threads on the other path are inactive;\nthe instruction is still executed for the whole warp,\nbut only the threads on this pass's path are allowed to\ntake effect. So every thread appears to take its own path.\n\nCost = the extra passes + the execution consumed by\nthe inactive threads in each pass.\n\nOne can determine if a control constructor can result in\nthread divergence by inspecting its decision condition. If\nthe decision condition is based on threadIdx values,\nthe control statement can potentially cause thread\ndivergence.\n\nExamples of thread divergence are if statements, such as\nif(threadIdx.x > 2) or if a loop condition is based on\nthread index values.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
